  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 103 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nFriday night through Wednesday:\n\nAn upper-level ridge is progged to gradually build across the \nUpper Midwest Saturday with the ridge axis gradually expected to\nslide east through the start of next week. However, the front \nthat has been the focus for pesky, scattered shower and \nthunderstorm activity through the end of the week looks to \nlinger for another day strewn across our southern half of the \nCWA into Saturday. Pair the surface boundary and hot, muggy \nairmass with a little bit of a ripple of mid- level vorticity, \nsome 700mb frontogenesis banding, and a 30-40 kt LLJ may be just\nenough forcing to support additional shower and thunderstorm \nchances for Saturday. The main factor affecting this activity \ninto Saturday will be the forcing aligning and the placement of \nthe surface boundary as it depends on prior days activity which \ncould end up pushing things further south into IL or may sit \nsomewhere across southern WI. Nevertheless, a potential will be \nthere.\n\nOtherwise, the rest of the weekend is trending drying as the \nridge slides further east and high pressure builds over the \nGreat Lakes region. Most models prog this high setting up to our\nnortheast which will increase easterly flow off of Lake \nMichigan. Thus, expecting some milder temps in the 70s to lower \n80s as well as lower dewpoints in the 60s for the end of the \nweekend into the start of next week. This pattern is set to \npersist through at least Tuesday as another high builds down \nacross central Canada into the Great Lakes and reinforce this \npattern along with easterly flow off of Lake Michigan. Then long\nrange models trends are favoring a warm up ahead of a trough \npassage through the middle of next week along with the potential\nfor additional shower and thunderstorm chances. \n\nWagner",
